          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of the
            %em smooth loop space
            of a (smooth)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} manifold
            is a way to make the set of smooth maps from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            to the target manifold into an object amenable to the tools of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth finite dimensional manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1 \to M
            is not usefully the underlying set of a finite dimensional manifold. Therefore to make it into an object that can be studied in differential topology, one has to allow for smooth spaces other than finite dimensional smooth manifolds. That is to say, one has to work in a larger category than just that of finite dimensional smooth manifolds.
          %p
            If the category is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            then the smooth loop space of a smooth space, say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            functor with that space as target and the circle as source:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L X \coloneqq [S^1, X]
          %p
            (This is the smooth
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space"} free loop space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loop+space"} based loop space
            one applies the standard restriction.)
          %p
            There are a variety of suitable categories listed at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
          %p
            The categories of smooth spaces discussed at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            are all cartesian closed and thus all admit smooth loop spaces of all objects. However, by restricting to a subcategory that might not be cartesian closed (for example, finite dimensional smooth manifolds) one can ask for another subcategory that is large enough to contain all the resulting smooth loop spaces. For finite dimensional smooth manifolds it turns out that one can give their smooth loop spaces the structure of an infinite dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
          %p
            Depending on taste and application, certain smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+objects"} quotient objects
            of this free loop space may be and are often considered.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#FrechetManifoldStructure
            Fréchet manifold structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
